WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.630 --> 00:00:05.540
The agents of the Naval Criminal Investigative
Service — or NCIS — often have to make

00:00:05.540 --> 00:00:09.660
snap decisions when in the middle of dangerous
and intense situations.

00:00:09.660 --> 00:00:13.480
Whether for the sake of their colleagues or
their country, several characters on the CBS

00:00:13.480 --> 00:00:19.179
procedural commit morally ambiguous actions
over the course of its staggering 19 seasons

00:00:19.179 --> 00:00:20.949
and 400-plus episodes.

00:00:20.949 --> 00:00:25.820
However, when the sunny goth forensics investigator
Abby — played by Pauley Perrette — breaks

00:00:25.820 --> 00:00:30.380
bad in a Season 5 episode, many fans consider
her behavior controversial.

00:00:30.380 --> 00:00:36.210
While Agents Gibbs, DiNozzo and David — portrayed
by Mark Harmon, Michael Weatherly, and Cote

00:00:36.210 --> 00:00:40.050
de Pablo, respectively — are characters
which openly struggle with personal demons

00:00:40.050 --> 00:00:44.280
or family complications, Abby is a generous
and playful person.

00:00:44.280 --> 00:00:48.320
But everything seems to take a sudden turn
in the episode "Dog Tags."

00:00:48.320 --> 00:00:53.329
The episode kicks off with the team executing
a warrant on a house, only to find their suspect

00:00:53.329 --> 00:00:55.030
mauled to death inside.

00:00:55.030 --> 00:00:59.560
The suspect's dog Butch then attacks Agent
McGee — portrayed by Sean Murray — landing

00:00:59.560 --> 00:01:02.180
a serious bite on his neck and wrist.

00:01:02.180 --> 00:01:06.270
McGee defends himself by non-fatally shooting
the canine, but Butch's behavior convinces

00:01:06.270 --> 00:01:09.759
the rest of the NCIS team that he killed the
suspect.

00:01:09.759 --> 00:01:10.899
All except Abby.

00:01:10.899 --> 00:01:15.469
Furious with McGee, Abby is convinced that
the dog — who she affectionately renames

00:01:15.469 --> 00:01:19.460
Jethro — did nothing wrong, and she risks
her career to prove his innocence.

00:01:19.460 --> 00:01:24.359
In 2019, on a comment thread titled "What
the Hell Abby?" appeared on the subreddit

00:01:24.359 --> 00:01:29.439
for NCIS — a community which hosts over
12,600 members as of 2022.

00:01:29.439 --> 00:01:32.719
A user took umbrage with an early scene in
the episode.

00:01:32.719 --> 00:01:36.709
During the scene in question, Abby berates
McGee when she learns he shot the dog, and

00:01:36.709 --> 00:01:39.999
doesn't believe him when he tries to explain
the severity of the attack.

00:01:39.999 --> 00:01:43.569
While the commenter felt that Abby meant well,
they also claimed that the dog:

00:01:43.569 --> 00:01:47.759
"[...] almost tore [McGee's] arm off and gave
him a life-long fear of dogs but she doesn't

00:01:47.759 --> 00:01:51.649
show the slightest bit of sympathy to her
friend, and she never apologizes."

00:01:51.649 --> 00:01:56.539
Numerous other Redditors affirmed that Abby's
treatment of McGee made little sense, considering

00:01:56.539 --> 00:02:00.070
the characters' existing professional and
personal relationships.

00:02:00.070 --> 00:02:02.229
Another user commented on the thread, saying
that Abby:

00:02:02.229 --> 00:02:06.999
"...forces (more like threatens) McGee to
actually contribute for flowers for the dog.

00:02:06.999 --> 00:02:09.780
AND LATER FORCES HIM TO TAKE HIM IN AS A PET."

00:02:09.780 --> 00:02:13.080
Another commenter referred to a scene where
Abby tells McGee:

00:02:13.080 --> 00:02:17.640
"Sometimes, you have to look at the reality
in front of you, and just accept it."

00:02:17.640 --> 00:02:22.010
The user points out the irony of these words
— since Abby doesn't follow her own advice,

00:02:22.010 --> 00:02:24.640
despite McGee telling Abby the truth about
what happened.

00:02:24.640 --> 00:02:25.640
They exclaim:

00:02:25.640 --> 00:02:30.530
"[...] this dog she just f---ing met is more
important than her friend, coworker, and ex-lover."

00:02:30.530 --> 00:02:34.910
General consensus seems to be that even though
Abby eventually proves the dog's innocence,

00:02:34.910 --> 00:02:39.910
that doesn't mean McGee should have to adopt
a semi-hostile animal who attacked him.

00:02:39.910 --> 00:02:43.970
In fact, numerous fans revealed how Abby's
attitude in this particular episode started

00:02:43.970 --> 00:02:48.290
her transformation into a selfish, petulant
person they didn't enjoy watching.

00:02:48.290 --> 00:02:52.820
An August 2021 post on the NCIS subreddit
includes a comment from user who wrote:

00:02:52.820 --> 00:02:59.230
"Cracked'' and “Dog Tags” [are] the quintessential
"Abby is a petulant child just cuz” episodes."

00:02:59.230 --> 00:03:04.290
Just a few months earlier, an entire thread
dedicated to "Dog Tags" popped up on the NCIS

00:03:04.290 --> 00:03:09.110
subreddit, the user wrote at length about
how the entire episode made them dislike Abby.

00:03:09.110 --> 00:03:14.231
They voiced distaste for Abbey's "highly unprofessional"
attitude when the time came to surrender the

00:03:14.231 --> 00:03:19.880
dog, and went on to detail their distaste
for how Abby completely neglects McGee's feelings.

00:03:19.880 --> 00:03:24.470
The post elicited a comment from another user
that appears to best sum up the entire issue

00:03:24.470 --> 00:03:26.720
with "Dog Tags" and the way it depicts Abby:

00:03:26.720 --> 00:03:31.410
"I always loved early seasons Abby but as
the show progressed I felt like she got more

00:03:31.410 --> 00:03:33.860
selfish, childish, and unprofessional.

00:03:33.860 --> 00:03:37.790
It was a weird regression, like they tried
too hard to make her quirky."

